{"name":"Earthen Jug","hex":"#a85e39","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"earthen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of earth or mud.","Made of clay."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["to provide or add soil to","to make earthly or earthlike"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/earthen"},{"term":"jug","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A serving vessel or container, typically circular in cross-section and typically higher than it is wide, with a relatively small mouth or spout, an ear handle and often a stopper or top.","The amount that a jug can hold."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To stew in an earthenware jug etc.","To put into jail."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jug"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jug","blocks":[{"text":"A jug is a type of container commonly used to hold and serve liquids, but not normally to drink from directly. It has an opening, sometimes narrow, from which to pour or drink, and has a handle, and usually a pouring lip. Jugs throughout history have been made of metal, ceramic, or glass, and plastic is now common."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/M0354_990116-6_1_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-M0354_990116-6_1_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jug"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/M0354_990116-6_1_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-M0354_990116-6_1_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/M0354_990116-6_1_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-M0354_990116-6_1_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"French ceramic jug","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AM0354_990116-6_1_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/